erviewed  vladimir putin the russian president um  now i think an overlooked storyline to  consider when conflict erupts abroad  just from a media perspective  is that the american news consumer is  watching war unfold through the  reporting of a select few correspondence  and so these kind of conflicts have the  ability to turn these reporters into  household names cnn's clarissa ward for  example was rightly celebrated for her  coverage of the withdrawal from  afghanistan um matthew chance who's also  a cnn reporter has been  on the ground in ukraine since before  the russian invasion and he's  distinguished himself with some urgent  and ubiquitous reporting uh about the  war he has been  stationed in kiev and reported on the  aftermath of bombings  he did a really memorable segment this  week from a bridge northwest of the  ukrainian capital  after a fierce gun fight between russian  and ukrainian troops  and then he landed this interview with  the ukrainian president of vladimir  zelinski  from his bunker in kiev  and it's kind of brave on the ground  reporting from around the world that  people look to cnn for um and i think  it's what incoming leadership of cnn has  said that they want from the network um  so cnn has a large group of reporters  and anchors stationed in ukraine  anderson cooper is now anchoring his  show live from ukraine  but it's really this reporting from  matthew chance that stood out this week  and as you point out the new leadership  is emphasizing news capital letters  instead of opinion that's right and you  know i think for the past couple years  cnn has gotten a reputation and not a  particularly good one uh for doing  leaning into uh opinion of the  anti-trump variety um that is cheap to  produce because all you have to do is  put a couple of pundits up on a panel  and have them uh fight it out um this  kind of reporting uh that cnn is really  known for and that does better than i'd  say any other network are reporting from  around t